crabbucket -- design notes
==========================

This file is the opinionated concept.  It is meant to be argued with.


1.  The thesis
--------------

A site is a typed value.

Every static site generator in wide use is a string pipeline.  Templates
render strings.  Frontmatter is a loose map.  Links are strings.  Design
tokens are strings.  The base path is a string.  Every one of those is a
place where a small site breaks in a way you only find in production: a
dead link, a page missing a title, a colour that was renamed six months
ago, a stylesheet 404ing because the site is served from /repo/ and not
from /.

Rust's advantage here is not speed.  Any generator is fast enough for a
forty-page docs site; Hugo builds it in 30ms and nobody is unhappy.  This
is not a hedge: on 521 generated pages crabbucket takes 250ms, Hugo 145ms
and Zola 314ms, so Hugo is about twice as fast and that is simply the
result (`bench/`, and the Speed page on the site).  The advantage is that
Rust can make the whole site a *typed value*, so that entire class of bug
stops being a runtime surprise and becomes a compile error.

  The build either fails, or the site is correct.

That is the whole product.  Everything below is in service of it.


2.  What is borrowed, and from where
------------------------------------

  Astro       content collections; zero JS by default; layouts; islands.
              Keep the model.  Drop the file format -- see section 4.
  Next.js     file-based routing; client navigation with no full reload.
  Zola/Hugo   one binary; `+++' TOML frontmatter; builds that feel instant.
  SvelteKit   prerender by default; a real answer for `base' paths.
  Style
  Dictionary  design tokens as the single source of truth, compiled out
              to every consumer rather than copied by hand.
  maud        HTML as a typed expression, not a string.
  htmx        the ~1KB "just swap the body" router, and nothing more.
  turborust   the dev loop.  Do not rebuild it; see section 8.


3.  Typed routes, and checked links
-----------------------------------

Routing is file-based over `content/'.  A file's path below that directory
is its route; any `index.md' takes the route of its directory; every route
is written as a directory holding an `index.html', so every URL ends in a
slash and no static host needs rewrite rules.

There are two halves to making links trustworthy, and they are not the
same problem.

**Components have a compiler.**  A site that is a crate generates a
`Route' enum from its content tree in `build.rs', so that

  a href=(Url::new(config, Route::DocsRouting.path())) { "Routing" }

does not compile if the page is renamed.  Two routes that would share a
variant name fail the build naming both, because picking one silently
would mean a link that compiles and goes to the wrong page.

The generator is `crabbucket-routes', which has no dependencies and is
also where `crabbucket' itself gets its answer to "what route is this
file?".  One implementation, used at build time and at run time, because
two of them would eventually disagree.

**Content does not.**  There is no compiler inside a `.md' file, so a
Markdown link cannot be typed at all.  The next best thing -- and the
thing that actually ships today -- is to make a dead internal link fail
the build:

  crab: 2 dead internal links:
    content/docs/routing.md: layout/ -> /docs/layout/ does not exist
    content/index.md: /repo/faq/ -> /faq/ does not exist

It runs after every page is rendered, because a link is dead only
relative to the finished set of routes.  It is not a warning and there is
no flag to disable it: a link checker you can turn off is a link checker
that is off.  External links are not checked -- their liveness is not a
property of this build, and a generator that phones the network to decide
whether it succeeded is a generator that fails on an aeroplane.

`url()' is also the only place the base path is applied.  A GitHub Pages
project site lives at `/crabbucket/', a user site or custom domain at
`/'; the difference is one line of config and no page ever knows about
it.  The single most common way these sites break is gone by
construction rather than by discipline.

4.  Typed content
-----------------

Content is Markdown with `+++' TOML frontmatter, in `content/'.  Writing
a page requires no Rust.

  +++
  title = "Getting started"
  order = 1
  +++

  Install it:

  :::callout{kind="note"}
  crabbucket needs Rust 1.88 or newer.
  :::

A collection is declared with the struct its frontmatter deserializes
into:

  #[derive(Deserialize)]
  struct DocMeta { title: String, order: u32 }

  let docs: Collection<DocMeta> = site.collection("docs")?;

A missing `title' is a build error naming the file and line.  A
collection is a `Vec' of typed entries, so sorting, grouping and
filtering are ordinary Rust rather than template-language contortions.
This is Astro's content collections, moved from runtime validation to
build-time typing.

Why not `.astro' files?  Because the appealing part of Astro is the
model, and the expensive part is the compiler.  A single-file component
format needs a parser, scoped-style extraction, and -- the killer -- an
evaluator for expressions inside the template.  In Rust that means
either shipping an interpreter or generating code from a bespoke syntax,
and both are strictly worse than writing Rust.  So: Astro's ergonomics
for content, Rust for components, and directives as the bridge.


5.  Components are functions
----------------------------

Components are `maud' functions.  HTML is an expression:

  pub fn callout(kind: Kind, body: Markup) -> Markup {
      html! {
          aside class={ "callout callout--" (kind.slug()) } {
              div.callout__body { (body) }
          }
      }
  }

Typed props.  Compile-checked structure.  No new language, no parser, no
runtime -- it compiles down to a `write!' chain, so rendering costs
approximately nothing.

Markdown reaches these functions through directives.  `:::callout{...}'
in a `.md' file resolves to that exact function, with its attributes
deserialized into the function's props.  An unknown directive, or a bad
attribute, fails the build.  That is MDX's expressiveness without owning
MDX's compiler.


6.  The design system is the core, not a plugin
-----------------------------------------------

`design/tokens.toml' is the source of truth:

  [color]
  surface = "#0b0d10"
  accent  = "#e8623c"

  [space]
  scale = [0, 4, 8, 12, 16, 24, 32, 48]

A build script generates, from that one file, both Rust constants and
CSS custom properties.  `tok::color::ACCENT' fails to compile if the
token is renamed, and the CSS variable can never drift from the Rust
constant because a single generator emits both.

Components declare their own styles.  An earlier draft of this document
promised that the build would collect the styles of components actually
rendered and ship only those; that promise is withdrawn.  On a
forty-page docs site the whole stylesheet is a few kilobytes, and
tree-shaking it optimises the wrong number.

The problem worth solving is not size but **collision**: two component
crates that both style `.card'.  That is a naming mechanism, not a build
pipeline, and it is cheaper by an order of magnitude.  See section 11.

A design system is a crate.  `crabbucket-ui' is the default one.  Your
own is a private crate that every one of your repo sites depends on, so
restyling all of them is a version bump -- which is the actual reason to
want first-class design systems on a fleet of small sites.


7.  Fast by construction, enhanced by choice
--------------------------------------------

The output is plain static HTML and CSS.  Zero JavaScript, works in a
text browser, perfect Lighthouse without trying.

Two things are opt-in, per site, and each ships nothing at all when it is
not asked for:

  router = true    a client-side navigation, tab memory, contents
                   highlighting, copy buttons and prefetching
  search = true    a search index and the client that reads it

The router intercepts same-origin clicks, fetches, swaps `<main>', pushes
history, and drives the native View Transitions API for the cross-fade.
It also moves focus into the new content, announces the new title through
a polite live region, honours fragments, and restores scroll position on
Back -- because a client-side navigation that does none of those is a
downgrade on the full page load it replaced.  It prefetches on hover
after 65ms, which measurement says is worth 110 to 260ms of a 230ms page
fetch from a CDN.

That is 2.9KB gzipped, not the "about a kilobyte" this section claimed
for its first three revisions.  The claim has been corrected rather than
the features quietly dropped, and there is a size budget in the test
suite so the number cannot grow again unnoticed.  It is still an order of
magnitude below any framework, and everything in it degrades to ordinary
navigation with scripting off, because it is ordinary navigation with
handlers in front of it.

Wasm islands are a later escape hatch, deliberately not in v0.  A landing
page and a docs site do not need them, and adding them early would warp
every other decision.


8.  The dev loop is turborust
-----------------------------

crabbucket ships no dev server, because turborust already is one:

  [tasks.site]
  cargo = "crabbucket-cli"
  cmd   = "cargo run -p crabbucket-cli -- build examples/site"

  [services.web]
  depends_on = ["site"]
  serve = { dir = "examples/site/dist", port = 8790 }

turborust derives the watch globs from the crate's path-dependency
closure, content-hashes the inputs, reruns the build only when something
really changed, serves `dist/' with live reload, and puts rustc
diagnostics in a browser overlay.  Writing a second, worse version of
that inside crabbucket would be the least defensible thing in this
document.

The dogfooding is therefore structural rather than decorative:
crabbucket's own site is built by crabbucket and developed under
turborust, and both projects are load-bearing for the other.


9.  Layouts are types
---------------------

Every generator lets a page name its layout in frontmatter, and every
generator resolves that name at render time.  Misspell it and you get a
fallback, or a warning in a log nobody reads.  It is the frontmatter
equivalent of a dead link, and it deserves the same treatment.

So `Theme' carries an associated type:

  pub trait Theme {
      type Layout: DeserializeOwned + Default;
      fn render(&self, page: &Page<'_, Self::Layout>) -> String;
      …
  }

and `PageMeta<L>' is generic over it, so a page's `layout' field
deserializes directly into the theme's own enum:

  unknown variant `dcos', expected one of `page', `docs', `landing'

That message is serde's, for free, because the layout was a type the
whole time.  There is no registry to maintain, no list of valid names to
keep in sync, and no code in crabbucket that knows the word "docs".
`render' then matches on it, which is exhaustive, so adding a layout
tells you where to handle it.

The same argument is why a theme is handed a `SiteIndex' rather than a
finished navigation list.  A docs site wants two views of the same set --
a masthead and a section sidebar -- and only the theme knows which.


10.  Style locality without a bundler
-------------------------------------

Replacing the withdrawn tree-shaking promise from section 6.

A component's styles should live next to the component, and two crates
should not be able to fight over a class name.  Both are achievable
without anything that deserves the word "bundler".

A `Style' carries a namespace and a name, and its CSS is written with `&'
standing in for the component's own class root:

  pub const CALLOUT: Style = Style::new(NS, "callout", "
      .& { border-left: 3px solid var(--color-accent); }
      .&--warn { border-left-color: var(--color-accent); }
      .&__body > :last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }
  ");

  CALLOUT.class()           // cb-callout
  CALLOUT.with("warn")      // cb-callout cb-callout--warn
  CALLOUT.element("body")   // cb-callout__body

which is BEM with the block name factored out, and the block name
namespaced in exactly one place.  The theme composes a `StyleSheet' from
the components it declares, deduplicating by namespace and name together,
so two design systems may both have a `card'.

An earlier draft of this section called for a `css!' macro.  There is no
macro: the substitution is a string replacement, the guarantee comes from
the namespace being impossible to omit, and a macro would have added a
compile-time dependency to buy nothing.

A small fixed set of class names belongs to the framework rather than to
any theme -- `heading-anchor', `code', and the `tok-' scope classes the
highlighter emits.  A theme styles them and may not rename them; they are
listed in `crabbucket::style::FRAMEWORK_CLASSES' so the rule is checkable
rather than remembered.

What this deliberately does not buy: dead-CSS elimination.  If a site ever
grows a stylesheet large enough for that to matter, the right response is
to look at what went wrong upstream of the stylesheet.


11.  Deliberately not in v0
---------------------------

  wasm islands, i18n, image optimisation, a plugin system, incremental
  builds, MDX-style arbitrary expressions in Markdown.

In v1, in roughly this order: RSS, and OpenGraph image generation.


12.  The command surface
------------------------

  crab build [DIR]  build to dist/, refusing to finish on a dead link
  crab dev          a shim that points at `turborust up' and exits 2
  crab serve        the same shim, for the same reason

There is no separate `crab check'.  Checking that used to be its own
command is now part of `crab build', which is the only way it stays
switched on.  `crab new' is not built yet.


13.  A note on this document
----------------------------

Sections 3, 6, 7, 10 and 12 have all been revised since the first draft,
in each case because building the thing showed the first answer to be
wrong.  Two of those revisions removed work rather than adding it, one
corrected a number the document had been repeating without measuring, and
one -- section 10 -- replaced a macro that turned out not to be needed
with fifteen lines that were.

The document is meant to be argued with, including by whoever is
implementing it, and a design note that has never been overruled by
contact with the code is a design note nobody was reading.
